## Minimal change for multi-file drag-and-drop

The root cause is in parse-jdx.js: the script currently does this:

```js
const inputPath = process.argv[2];
```

That means it only accepts one path. In Windows Explorer, dropping multiple files usually produces something like:

```text
app.exe file1.html file2.html file3.html
```

and Node exposes all of them in `process.argv.slice(2)`.

The smallest safe fix is:

1. Collect all non-flag arguments.
2. Validate each one.
3. Process them in a loop.
4. Keep the same output naming pattern: each file gets its own sibling output.

---

## Suggested change

Replace the current top of `main()` with logic like this:

```js
async function main() {
  let exitCode = 0;
  const filesToProcess = [];

  try {
    const rawArgs = process.argv.slice(2);
    const flags = new Set(["--debug", "--no-pause"]);

    // Ignore known flags; keep all remaining file paths.
    const inputPaths = rawArgs.filter((arg) => !flags.has(arg));

    if (!inputPaths.length) {
      throw new Error(
        "No input file was provided.\n\n" +
        "Drag one or more JDX HTML files onto JDX-Accessible-Converter.exe."
      );
    }

    for (const inputPath of inputPaths) {
      if (!fs.existsSync(inputPath)) {
        console.warn(`Skipping missing file: ${inputPath}`);
        continue;
      }

      const extension = path.extname(inputPath).toLowerCase();
      if (extension !== ".html" && extension !== ".htm") {
        console.warn(`Skipping non-HTML file: ${inputPath}`);
        continue;
      }

      filesToProcess.push(inputPath);
    }

    if (!filesToProcess.length) {
      throw new Error("No valid HTML files were supplied.");
    }

    for (const inputPath of filesToProcess) {
      const outputPath =
        process.argv.includes("--output") ? /* optional */ outputPathForInput(inputPath)
        : defaultOutputPath(inputPath);

      const source = fs.readFileSync(inputPath, "utf8");
      const model = parseJdx(source);
      const output = renderAccessibleHtml(model);
      const warnings = audit(model);

      fs.writeFileSync(outputPath, output, "utf8");

      console.log("");
      console.log(`Converted: ${inputPath}`);
      console.log(`Created: ${outputPath}`);
      console.log(`Working title: ${model.workingTitle || "(not found)"}`);
      console.log(`Classification: ${model.classificationTitle || "(not found)"}`);

      if (warnings.length) {
        console.warn(`Warnings for ${inputPath}:`);
        for (const warning of warnings) {
          console.warn(`- ${warning}`);
        }
      }
    }
  } catch (error) {
    exitCode = 1;
    console.error("");
    console.error("The file could not be converted.");
    console.error("");
    console.error(error.message);

    if (process.argv.includes("--debug") && error.stack) {
      console.error("");
      console.error(error.stack);
    }
  } finally {
    await pauseBeforeExit();
    process.exitCode = exitCode;
  }
}
```

---

## Small helper to keep it clean

You can add this helper:

```js
function outputPathForInput(inputPath, forcedOutputPath) {
  if (forcedOutputPath) return forcedOutputPath;
  return defaultOutputPath(inputPath);
}
```

Or, if you want the simplest possible change with no new helper, just keep the existing `defaultOutputPath(inputPath)` inside the loop.

---

## Why this is the minimum-change approach

- It preserves the current single-file behavior.
- It keeps the Windows Explorer drag-and-drop workflow unchanged.
- It only changes the argument handling and wraps the conversion in a loop.
- It does not require a different UI or packaging approach.

> If you drop multiple files, Explorer will still pass them as separate arguments; the script just needs to treat them as a list instead of a single value.

---

## One caution

If a user drops a mix of valid and invalid files, this pattern is usually better than failing the entire batch:

- valid HTML files are converted
- invalid files are skipped with a warning
- the app exits with a nonzero code only if nothing valid was processed

That is the most user-friendly behavior for Explorer drag-and-drop.

