## Yes — this is a very reasonable extension, and it can be done with a small change in parse-jdx.js

The key is to stop treating `process.argv[2]` as “the one input.” Instead, treat the arguments as a list of paths, then expand any directory arguments into HTML files.

---

## Minimal pattern

Use this logic:

```js
const rawArgs = process.argv.slice(2).filter(arg => !arg.startsWith("--"));
const inputFiles = expandInputArgs(rawArgs);
```

Then implement:

```js
function expandInputArgs(args) {
  const files = [];

  for (const arg of args) {
    if (!arg) continue;

    if (!fs.existsSync(arg)) {
      console.warn(`Skipping missing path: ${arg}`);
      continue;
    }

    const stat = fs.statSync(arg);

    if (stat.isDirectory()) {
      const entries = fs.readdirSync(arg, { withFileTypes: true });

      for (const entry of entries) {
        if (!entry.isFile()) continue;

        const lower = entry.name.toLowerCase();
        if (lower.endsWith(".html") || lower.endsWith(".htm")) {
          files.push(path.join(arg, entry.name));
        }
      }

      continue;
    }

    const ext = path.extname(arg).toLowerCase();
    if (ext === ".html" || ext === ".htm") {
      files.push(arg);
    } else {
      console.warn(`Skipping non-HTML file: ${arg}`);
    }
  }

  return files;
}
```

This supports:

- one file dropped
- multiple files dropped
- a directory dropped
- a mix of files and folders

---

## Batch output folder behavior

For your requirement, if there are multiple valid files, create a subfolder in the same directory as those files.

The easiest version is:

```js
function determineOutputPath(inputFile, isBatch) {
  if (!isBatch) {
    return defaultOutputPath(inputFile);
  }

  const dir = path.dirname(inputFile);
  const batchDir = path.join(dir, "accessible-output");

  if (!fs.existsSync(batchDir)) {
    fs.mkdirSync(batchDir, { recursive: true });
  }

  const parsed = path.parse(inputFile);
  return path.join(batchDir, `${parsed.name}.accessible.html`);
}
```

